<h1>heap property</h1>
<p>
(definition)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
Each <a href="node.html"><em>node</em></a> in a <a href="tree.html"><em>tree</em></a> has a <a href="key.html"><em>key</em></a> which is more extreme (greater or less) than or equal to the key of its  <a href="parent.html"><em>parent</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Specialization</strong> (... is a kind of me.)<br>
<a href="maxheapprop.html"><em>max-heap property</em></a>, <a href="minheapprop.html"><em>min-heap property</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>Aggregate parent</strong> (I am a part of or used in ...)<br>
<a href="binaryheap.html"><em>binary heap</em></a>, <a href="heap.html"><em>heap</em></a>, <a href="binaryPriorityQueue.html"><em>binary priority queue</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
The <a href="root.html"><em>root</em></a> node has the largest or smallest key. After LK. </p> 
<p> A <a href="completetree.html"><em>complete tree</em></a> with the heap property is a <a href="heap.html"><em>heap</em></a>.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>
<h2>More information</h2>
<p>
An <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=H5kAcmGOn4Q&t=31" target="_blank">illustration</a> of what heap property means followed by how it is used in help sort. <br /> <iframe src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/H5kAcmGOn4Q?start=31" frameborder="0" allowfullscreen></iframe>
</p>

